o make the splits before the  launch so this is how we resolved the  issue  the feds founded just enough queries to  allow the load based split to trigger  and increase the number of queries  gradually until there was enough splits  to handle the desired tps  this was a tricky process because if we  fed it too many  the cpu would starve which slowed down  the splitting process  but if we gave it too little there  wouldn't be enough load to trigger the  split  so we had to monitor the metrics  constantly to ensure that we fed spanner  the right amount of queries  since this was a tedious task that took  hours to complete  we made a warm-up app that automated the  whole process  and here's an illustration of what the  whole process looks like  so in addition to what i've just  described there are two things you  should  be aware of when doing a warm-up  so number one is do not drop the tables  after the warm-up  in most cases you would want to delete  dummy records that were inserted  during the warm-up but don't do it by  dropping the tables because that will  also clear the splits you should delete  individual records instead  since that will allow the splits to  remain so the second thing you should  know is  to warm up 24 hours before the launch  spanner seems to merge the splits if  they aren't being accessed for over 48  hours  so make sure you warm up your database  48 hours  prior to launch so the last thing i want  to advise  is how to scale in so in this game we  scaled in and out  fairly often based on how much traffic  we're expecting for the day  scaling out is simple since all you have  to do is increase the number of nodes in  the ui  and wait a few minutes for it to be  deployed scaling in however  is a bit more tricky when we scale in by  reducing the number of nodes  too aggressively we notice that the  latency increase for some users  these latencies range from a few  milliseconds up to something like 30  seconds  so we resolve this issue by forcing  spanner to time out in three seconds  and return a retry response which asks  the client to retry after a few seconds  in addition to that we now reduce the  number of nodes in small intervals  since the latency seems to be  proportional to the number of nodes we  reduced  we managed to minimize the delay enough  no retries occurred  on the client by reducing the number to  one node per five minutes  so the takeaway here is don't try to  reduce the number of nodes in one go  but reduce them gradually in small  intervals that's all i have for you  today  thank you for your time handing it back  to you sofia  thank you taka for your great story  for the next steps we have a few more  sessions about spanner so if you have  time  please please watch them as well also  we have demo and quick lab about spanner  as well  please try them out okay that's all for  today from us  thank you for watching
 

